WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.315
As autonomous AI agents take over on-chain trading and data pipelines, they run into two missing primitives.

2
00:00:06.315 --> 00:00:13.256
First: how do they pay for specialized micro-services without centralized credit cards or custodial API accounts?

3
00:00:13.256 --> 00:00:20.339
And second: how do downstream contracts verify that an agent's audit was actually computed against canonical on-chain state?

4
00:00:20.339 --> 00:00:22.719
This is Sentinel402 Gateway.

5
00:00:22.719 --> 00:00:31.853
An autonomous liquidity auditor powered by the x402 Micropayment Protocol, The Graph Subgraph Studio, and ENSv2 permissioned attestations.

6
00:00:31.853 --> 00:00:35.855
Let's see what happens when an unauthorized agent requests an on-demand audit.

7
00:00:35.855 --> 00:00:45.449
When we trigger the endpoint without payment, the gateway immediately intercepts the call and returns an RFC-compliant HTTP 402 Payment Required challenge.

8
00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:54.506
Notice the response: it specifies the exact micropayment required — 0.001 ETH — along with the Bazantic facilitator address.

9
00:00:54.506 --> 00:00:57.138
No credit cards, no pre-shared secrets.

10
00:00:57.138 --> 00:01:01.644
Any autonomous agent can parse this header and settle payment programmatically.

11
00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:06.644
Now, we pass a valid Bazantic MPP session token and re-execute.

12
00:01:06.644 --> 00:01:12.872
The gateway verifies the credential against the facilitator and dispatches Step Two of our declarative Bazantic recipe.

13
00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:19.714
Here, Sentinel402 queries The Graph Subgraph Studio for live mainnet Uniswap v3 pool data.

14
00:01:19.714 --> 00:01:27.807
In real time, it indexes total value locked, fee tiers, and volume velocity, calculating a comprehensive liquidity risk score.

15
00:01:27.807 --> 00:01:40.230
Most importantly, the agent serializes this state into a canonical payload and computes a deterministic Keccak-256 report hash, creating a tamper-proof cryptographic fingerprint of the audited liquidity.

16
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.695
In Step Three, we lock this audit permanently on-chain.

17
00:01:43.695 --> 00:01:57.182
Using ENSv2 on Ethereum Sepolia, the agent connects to the official Permissioned Resolver and calls setText, binding the audit hash directly to the subname auditor.sentinel402.eth, under the key records['last_audit_hash'].

18
00:01:57.182 --> 00:02:06.962
Any DeFi protocol or smart contract can now inspect this ENS record to verify that liquidity depth was independently validated before routing high-value transactions.

19
00:02:06.962 --> 00:02:13.607
For AI agent developers, Sentinel402 exposes this entire lifecycle over the Model Context Protocol.

20
00:02:13.607 --> 00:02:25.131
Tools like Claude Desktop, Cursor, or autonomous agent frameworks can call audit_pool_liquidity or get ENS attestation natively via stdio or HTTP JSON-RPC.

21
00:02:25.131 --> 00:02:35.449
We've also built an interactive Demo Studio right into the frontend, letting judges replay every stage, inspect raw payloads, and export signed audit runs.

22
00:02:35.449 --> 00:02:47.982
Sentinel402 Gateway brings together machine-payable APIs, real-time decentralized indexing, and verifiable on-chain identity, building the trust layer for the next generation of autonomous on-chain agents.

23
00:02:47.982 --> 00:02:54.232
Thank you, and check out the open-source code on GitHub at nlnw/eth-online-2026.
